  :body "Clause 0, X0(e) and the kernel choice. Three of eleven reachable states have two incomparable patterns enabled at once. At theta 0.8, horizon 6: co-application reaches all wants with p 0.26; the list kernel (every linear extension) and interleaving reach them with p 0. At horizon 14 all three give 0.998-1.0. So the semilattice changes the RATE at which wants are reached, not whether they can be. X0(e) as written (flatten; if nothing changes, the semilattice did no work) BREAKS: on a click whose horizon exceeds the cascade's depth, flattening changes nothing whatever the structure. The finding has to be stated relative to the horizon. Interleaving tracks the list kernel, not co-application: the choice that matters is one pattern per step versus several. Instance 5 (after 3f72bdc2) adds a second way X0(e) misleads: at horizon 8 the list kernel reaches all wants with p between 0.168 and 0.797 across its 48 linear extensions, a factor of 4.7 set by an order nobody chose. A click that flattens and recomputes sees a plausible number, not an obviously wrong one. X0(e) should compare against the spread over linear extensions, not one flattening."}
